    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> founded the city on 27 May 1703 on land taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, using conscripted serfs, and moved the capital there in 1712.</li><li>It was the seat of the Russian court and government for most of the time until 1918, and the revolutions of 1905 and 1917 began there before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> moved the government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1918.</li><li>It was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> in 1914 and Leningrad in 1924, survived a siege of 872 days from 1941 to 1944, and took back the name Saint Petersburg in 1991.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> renamed the city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> to remove the German words Sankt and Burg from its name. It became Leningrad in 1924, and a referendum in 1991 restored the name Saint Petersburg.</p></aside>
    </section>

