    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1703</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> captures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyenskans">Nyenskans</a> and lays down the Peter and Paul Fortress</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1712</span><span class="rf-text">Peter moves the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to Saint Petersburg</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1825</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decembrists">Decembrist revolt</a> takes place on Senate Square</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1861</span><span class="rf-text">The emancipation of the serfs brings a large influx of former peasants into the capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> storm the Winter Palace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1918</span><span class="rf-text">Lenin moves the Soviet government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text">German forces begin the siege of Leningrad, which lasts until 1944</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1991</span><span class="rf-text">A referendum restores the name Saint Petersburg</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-the-great.jpg" alt="Peter the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inconnu_d%27apr%C3%A8s_J.-M._Nattier,_Portrait_de_Pierre_Ier_(mus%C3%A9e_de_l%E2%80%99Ermitage).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Jean-Marc Nattier, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saint Petersburg stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva River</a> at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>, on the islands of the river's delta and the lowlands beside Neva Bay. Before the 17th century the area was inhabited by Finnic Izhorians and Votians. Swedish colonists built the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyenskans">Nyenskans</a> at the mouth of the Neva in 1611, and the small town of Nyen grew up around it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingrian-finns">Ingrian Finns</a> moved to the region from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savonia">Savonia</a> during the Swedish rule, and there were also some Estonian, Karelian, Russian and German inhabitants.</p><p>At the end of the 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> wanted a seaport from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> could trade with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Arkhangelsk</a>, the country's main port, lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> in the far north and was closed to shipping in winter. On 12 May 1703 (1 May in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>), during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, Peter captured Nyenskans. On 27 May 1703 he laid down the Peter and Paul Fortress on Zayachy (Hare) Island, closer to the estuary, and it became the first brick and stone building of the new city. He named the city after his patron saint, the apostle Peter.</p><p>The city was built under harsh conditions by conscripted serfs from all over Russia. Peter ordered a yearly conscription of 40,000 serfs, and tens of thousands of them died in the work. The marshland was drained under German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> engineers whom Peter had invited to Russia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/burkhard-christoph-von-munnich"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burkhard-christoph-von-munnich.jpg" alt="Burkhard Christoph von Münnich" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/burkhard-christoph-von-munnich">Burkhard Christoph von Münnich</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minikh_BK.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heinrich Buchholz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Peter moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to Saint Petersburg in 1712, nine years before the Treaty of Nystad ended the war, and he had called it the capital as early as 1704. The city became a seaport, a naval base protected by the fortress of Kronstadt, and what Russians called the "window to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>". Peter restricted the building of stone houses in the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> so that stonemasons would come to the new city, and he hired engineers, architects, shipbuilders and merchants from across Europe. In 1716 Domenico Trezzini drew up a plan with the centre on Vasilyevsky Island and a rectangular grid of canals; it was not completed, but it shows in the layout of the streets. In 1724 the Academy of Sciences, the University and the Academic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a> were founded.</p><p>After the fires of 1736 and 1737 a committee under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkhard-christoph-von-munnich">Burkhard Christoph von Münnich</a> commissioned a new plan, which moved the centre to the Admiralty borough and set three radial streets running from the Admiralty, among them Nevsky Prospect. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> building reached its height with Rastrelli's Winter Palace, and neoclassical architecture followed from the 1760s. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> were lined with granite embankments.</p><p>After the emancipation of the serfs in 1861 and industrialisation, many former peasants came to the capital. The city passed Moscow in population and industrial growth, and its population stood at 2.4 million in 1916.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Saint Petersburg&#8221; cites where it covers Imperial era (1703–1917):</p><ul><li>Derek Wilson, Peter the Great (Macmillan, 2010)</li><li>Lindsey Hughes, Peter the Great: a Biography (Yale University Press, 2004)</li><li>Matthew S. Anderson, Peter the Great (Thames &amp; Hudson, 1978)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great.jpg" alt="Catherine the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catherine_II_after_Roslin,_Rokotov_(1780s,_Kunsthistorisches_Museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Alexander Roslin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The site belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> took it in 1703, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>. Aleksandr Menshikov, Peter's close friend, was the first governor general of the Saint Petersburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> from 1703 to 1727, and was arrested and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> after Peter's death in 1725. In 1728 Peter II moved the seat of government back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, but in 1732, under Empress Anna, Saint Petersburg became the capital again, and it remained the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov dynasty</a> and the government until 1917.</p><p>Several rulers met violent ends in or near the city. Peter III was supposedly killed in 1762 in a conspiracy led by his wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>, Paul I was assassinated in 1801 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> was killed by terrorists in 1881. In March 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> abdicated, ending over three hundred years of Romanov rule, and on 7 November 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> under Lenin stormed the Winter Palace. In March 1918 Lenin moved the Soviet government to Moscow, away from the border.</p><p>The city was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> in 1914 and Leningrad in 1924. In 1931 Leningrad was separated administratively from Leningrad Oblast. In 1991 Anatoly Sobchak became its first directly elected mayor, and the city again took the name Saint Petersburg. The Constitutional Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> moved to Senate Square in 2008.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Saint Petersburg&#8221; cites where it covers Imperial era (1703–1917), Revolution and Soviet era (1917–1941):</p><ul><li>Derek Wilson, Peter the Great (Macmillan, 2010)</li><li>Lindsey Hughes, Peter the Great: a Biography (Yale University Press, 2004)</li><li>Matthew S. Anderson, Peter the Great (Thames &amp; Hudson, 1978)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky.jpg" alt="Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tchaikovsky_by_Reutlinger_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Émile Reutlinger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The educated immigrants whom Peter brought made the city more cosmopolitan than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. The Kunstkamera, founded in 1714 to collect curiosities from all over the world, is sometimes considered the first museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The Saint Petersburg Conservatory was founded in 1862 by Anton Rubinstein, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a>, Prokofiev and Shostakovich were among its alumni. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a>, Gogol, Dostoyevsky and other writers set works in the city, and Dostoyevsky called it "the most abstract and intentional city in the world". The Mariinsky <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> became the home of a ballet company and opera, and dancers such as Nijinsky and Anna Pavlova were its principal stars.</p><p>The city was laid out around its water. Peter proposed boats as a means of transport in a city of canals, inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. At first only a few small bridges crossed the smaller waterways, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontoon-bridge">pontoon bridge</a> was built over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> each summer; the first permanent bridge over the Neva, the Annunciation Bridge, opened in 1850. Nevsky Prospect became the main street. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Lutheran and Catholic churches stood along it, and the Saint Petersburg Mosque, the largest mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> when it opened in 1913, stood on the right bank.</p><p>In the Soviet period many large apartments were divided among several families in communal flats, and by the 1930s 68 per cent of the population lived in such housing, under very poor conditions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city stood on low ground, and parts of it suffered repeated floods. The worst, in 1824, reached 4.21 metres above sea level and destroyed over 300 buildings. Fires in 1736 and 1737 were catastrophic. In 1825 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decembrists">Decembrist revolt</a> took place on Senate Square, a day after Nicholas I assumed the throne. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Revolution of 1905</a> began in the city. Between 1917 and the 1930s about two million people fled the city, and its population fell from 2.4 million in 1916 to under 740,000 by 1920. After Sergei Kirov was assassinated on 1 December 1934, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> followed, and approximately 40,000 people were executed in Leningrad in Stalin's purges. In the Leningrad affair of 1949 to 1952 the city's party leadership was destroyed, and 23 leaders were sentenced to death.</p><p>German forces besieged Leningrad from 8 September 1941 to 27 January 1944, 872 days. The city was shelled and bombed, and the only route for supplies was the Road of Life across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a>, which could be used only once the lake froze. On 20 November 1941 the bread ration fell to 250 grams for a worker and 125 grams for others. More than one million civilians were killed, mainly from starvation, and some accounts give about 1.2 million of 3 million. About 2,000 residents were arrested for cannibalism. The city's population fell from 3 million in 1941 to under 600,000 by the end of 1943.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Leningrad was named a Hero City in 1945 in Stalin's order of 1 May, and a law of 1965 confirmed the title. It received the Order of Lenin and the Gold Star medal for the resistance of the city and the endurance of those who survived the siege. The city was rebuilt over the following decades, partly according to prewar plans, and the Amber Room, looted by the Germans from the Catherine Palace, was re-created in 2003.</p><p>The centre of Saint Petersburg, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> and neoclassical buildings of the 18th and 19th centuries, has been largely preserved. Because the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, later large building projects tended to be put up there, which helped keep the old centre's appearance. Many historic churches and some other buildings were destroyed in the Soviet period for ideological reasons. Since 1991 the Historic Centre of Saint Petersburg and Related Groups of Monuments have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The Hermitage, which includes the Winter Palace, is one of the largest art museums in the world, and the Bronze Horseman, the equestrian monument to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> on Senate Square, is among the city's best-known symbols.</p><p>Saint Petersburg is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with about 5.6 million people in the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is a federal city, and from 1991 it again bears the name given to it by Peter the Great.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Saint Petersburg, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>, was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> in 1703 and was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> until 1918. It was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> and Leningrad, endured a siege of 872 days, and is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s second-largest city.</em></p>

